some surrounded by a protective membrane  which makes it difficult for viruses to  get inside them HIV is an enveloped  virus surrounded by a similar membrane  that is able to fuse with the cell  membrane the envelope glycoprotein of  HIV is performs the steps needed for  membrane fusion the env glycoprotein is  found on the surface of HIV inform swabs  may put three identical subunits the end  is covered with carbohydrate and is  encoded in the HIV genome as one long  protein the glycoprotein has been very  difficult to study because it is covered  in flexible carbohydrates it is normally  bound to membranes and it is naturally  flexible researchers have solved this  problem by cutting off all the flexible  and sticky parts and just turning the  rigid cord molecule the immune system  looks for foreign molecules in the body  but the HIV virus has learned to  disguise itself as a human molecule  making all the more difficult for our  immune system to find and destroy when  viruses make their surface glycoproteins  in infected cells they camouflage  themselves with same sugar chains that  coat the proteins of humans which makes  it hard for antibodies to reach these  viruses have error-prone replication  machinery which means that once the  immune system has found antibodies to  recognize the infecting virus other  viruses rush to rapidly mutate to chain  to the site that is recognized so the  immune system cannot destroy them  antibodies focus on molecules on the  viral surface that are bound to the  envelope glycoprotein this is a problem  because one the virus can quickly evade  the antibodies and two the antibodies  are attacking the surface which is not  essential for function of the virus  after some time fighting the HIV  infection some people develop broadly  neutralizing antibodies termed broadly  because they attack many strains of the  virus and neutralizing because they  attack key functional sites in the virus  and block infection  unfortunately however these antibodies  usually come too late and do not provide  effective protection above the disease  researchers are trying to make the  immune system create these broadly  neutralizing antibiotics quickly through  the use of a vaccine researchers have a  daunting task of creating this  life-saving vaccine but while it is in  development it is important to get  tested for HIV
 

